## 7. Open items

Grouped by what access each one needs — software, a DMM, the soldering iron, or the
rack with the Orion attached. See the sequencing note below for why that ordering
changed.

### Closed

- ~~**Measure AR-40A open-circuit output and source impedance.**~~ 2026-09-07:
  4.44 Vpp open, 46.2 Ω source, +11.25 dBm available.
- ~~**Fit the pad.**~~ 2026-09-07: DIY 7.01 dB pi, verified to 1% of prediction.
  Rebuilt 2026-09-09 with the same 150/51/150 values; confirmed indirectly by CLKIN
  measuring 2.1 Vpp clamped from an expected 2.49 Vpp, which is only reachable if the
  pad is delivering 1.245 Vpp into the DA.
- ~~**Resolve the AR-40A BIT polarity contradiction.**~~ Settled by the existing lock
  interlock — see §3.
- ~~**Check what the Si5351 breakout does at its SMA input.**~~ 2026-09-09: nothing.
  CLKIN sits at 0 V DC and clamps on the negative half (§8), so there is no bias
  network on the breakout.
- ~~**Verify the chain end to end.**~~ 2026-09-09: AR-40A → pad → DA R in → DA R out →
  CLKIN unterminated. PLLA locks, CLK4 reads 54.000 MHz, CM4 boots.
- ~~**Verify supply rails.**~~ 2026-09-09: all four within 23 mV, CM4 at 4.917 V
  booted (§5).

- ~~**Flash and verify the gating firmware.**~~ 2026-09-09: running. CLK4 gated on
  LOS_CLKIN / LOL_A; verified against a real AR-40A power cycle — reference lost,
  sticky 0xF0 confirming the drop was genuine, CLK4 disabled, then re-enabled ~1 s
  after the signal returned. BIT proven in both directions (LOCKED → UNLOCKED →
  LOCKED). Warm-up behaviour confirmed: with CLKIN present but BIT unlocked, CLK4
  stays enabled, so the CM4 is not held off during rubidium pull-in. Panel LEDs
  wired and working.

  Two bugs found and fixed along the way: a failed I2C read returned 0xFF and was
  indistinguishable from every fault bit set, so single bus glitches killed CLK4 for
  a second at a time — now a third state that holds the output; and a single-poll
  blip could disable, now debounced at two consecutive bad reads.

- ~~**Confirm the F9T breakout's I/O level.**~~ 2026-09-09: 3.3 V, confirmed by the
  UART decoding cleanly on GPIO15 (§3).
- ~~**Decide the F9T data link.**~~ 2026-09-09: UART1 on ttyAMA0 at 38400. USB
  rejected — the breakout ties header +5V to USB VBUS, and CM4 USB sits behind the
  VL805 over PCIe.
- ~~**Wire the F9T PPS.**~~ 2026-09-09: TIME2 → J2 pin 9 (SYNC_OUT), 47 Ω, shield at
  the CM4 end only. chrony sees pulses. Settled the pin-8-vs-9 question and the
  1.8 V-vs-3.3 V documentation conflict at the same time (§3).
- ~~**Cut the Teensy's VUSB pad.**~~ 2026-09-09. USB and the external 5 V can now
  coexist.
- ~~**Teensy → CM4 status link.**~~ 2026-09-09: Serial1 → ttyAMA5 on GPIO12/13,
  115200. Firmware reports to USB and Serial1 together (§3).
- ~~**Image the eMMC.**~~ 2026-09-09, via rpiboot mass-storage-gadget64. Motivated by
  discovering the kernel carries a non-standard BCM54210PE timestamping driver (§3).
  **Image verified readable 2026-09-10.** An unopened image is a hypothesis, not a
  backup; this one is now actually a backup of the irreproducible kernel.
- ~~**Drop `dtoverlay=miniuart-bt`.**~~ 2026-09-10. `disable-bt` kept (§3).
- ~~**I2C data integrity — fix flashed and its premise verified.**~~ 2026-09-10.
  Bus to 100 kHz; reg 20 read back at init and **verified 0x4F**, closing the CLK4
  drive-strength question for good; a fourth gating state for readings rejected by
  the reg0/reg1 sticky cross-check, counted as `dataErr` because `i2cErr` only
  counts failed transactions and was under-reporting.

  **`FAULT_MASK` is 0x30 — LOL_A and LOS_CLKIN only.** Measured: sticky reads 0xC0
  on every steady-state poll, i.e. SYS_INIT_STKY and LOL_B_STKY both re-latch
  immediately after each clear, so neither can ever contradict a live bit. A
  glitched reg0=0xC1 was observed sailing through the earlier 0xB0 mask with
  `dataErr=0` for exactly this reason. `[meas]`

  **The premise is confirmed, not assumed:** the first poll after init read
  `sticky=0xE0` — LOL_A_STKY latched while PLLA was acquiring — and every poll
  since reads 0xC0. So LOL_A_STKY latches on the event and stays clear once the
  condition is gone, which is what makes the cross-check meaningful rather than
  vacuous. `[meas]`

  Consequently **SYS_INIT is reported but no longer gated on**: its sticky cannot
  police it, so a single flipped bit 7 would otherwise be two polls from cutting the
  CM4's clock. Startup is still covered by the SYS_INIT wait in `si5351Init()`, and
  a genuine device reset shows on LOL_A too.

  Gating sequence confirmed on this build: `CLK4=off` on the first report, enabled
  on the fourth clean poll, steady thereafter.

### Sequencing — what the constraint actually is

The rack move is the last moment the box and the scope are on the same table, and
after it the Orion is available as a load. That used to imply a large "do it before
the move" batch. It no longer does:

- The CLK4/XIN amplitude question is deferred until a second CM4 exists (§8), so it
  is not a bench item at all.
- EXTINT is already on a BNC (§3), so no panel work is outstanding.
- The Teensy's USB is reachable from the rack, and its status text also reaches the
  CM4 on ttyAMA5, so **firmware iteration does not require pulling the box.**
- The AR-40A chassis-to-earth check is a property of the unit, not of the bench.

What is left before the move is therefore software, plus building two connectors.

### Open — today, software only

1. **Soak `dataErr`.** The firmware is flashed and working, but a handful of clean
   polls proves nothing — the 0xC1 event was roughly one in several hundred at
   400 kHz. Leave it running an hour or two and read the counter. Zero means the bus
   speed was the whole story and the pull-up question closes; anything above zero is
   the previously-invisible error rate finally being counted, and the answer is
   external 2.2k–4.7k to 3V3 on SDA/SCL. `[?]`
2. **`setClk4()` asserts rather than verifies.** It writes reg 3 and discards
   `siWrite()`'s return, so the log prints "CLK4: ENABLED" whether or not the write
   landed — the status line reports intent, not state. Read reg 3 back and report
   the actual OE register. Cost nothing today only because the clock turned out to
   be fine. `[plan]`
3. **Get a current `ubxtool`** onto the CM4 (single Python file into
   `/usr/local/bin`); the packaged one predates CFG-VALGET and returns nothing.
   Then do the read-backs in §6b — TMODE, TP2 both cases, existing CFG-TP5 cable
   delay. **Reads, not writes**: the surveyed position stays.
4. **Point gpsd at /dev/ttyAMA0.** chrony's NMEA refclock shows reach 0;
   `/etc/default/gpsd` most likely still names the old USB device. `[?]`
5. **No boot console.** The 2026-09-10 session was spent blind because GPIO14/15 is
   correctly occupied by the F9T, so there is nowhere to watch a boot. Everything
   diagnosed that day was diagnosed by inference. The Teensy has spare UARTs and a
   USB path to the bench, and is a candidate host for a console bridge — this
   overlaps the §6b consolidation question. `[plan]`

### Open — five minutes with a DMM, whenever the box is reachable

6. **Is the AR-40A chassis at mains PE?** Unplugged continuity, enclosure to inlet
   earth pin. Determines whether the 10 MHz chain has one earth injection or two
   (§5). This is a property of the unit — bench or rack makes no difference. `[?]`

### Open — build the connectors before the move

7. **DIY a 75 Ω terminator, a 50 Ω terminator and a BNC tee** from the chassis
   connectors already on hand. See the construction note below. Without a known-value
   terminator of his own, item 8's result is ambiguous between "the DA sags into a
   real load" and "the Orion doesn't terminate at 75 Ω" — the terminator is what
   makes that measurement mean anything, so this is not a stopgap for a bought part.

### Open — the rack move, one measurement session with the scope carried over

8. **DA output into a real 75 Ω load, and the Orion's actual termination — one
   procedure, three readings** at the Orion's input, same setup, same probe:
   1. Cable open → **V_open**, the DA's unloaded output.
   2. Known 75 Ω terminator on the free leg of the tee → if this reads ≈ V_open/2,
      the DA's build-out really is 75 Ω *and* it drives a real load without sagging.
      That closes the item-8 question on its own.
   3. Orion connected → with the source impedance now confirmed, the Orion's input
      resistance is 75 × V_orion / (V_open − V_orion). ~1.26 V against ~2.5 V open
      means it terminates at 75 Ω.

   Measured clean unloaded already (§3), which rules out voltage clipping and slew
   limiting; what is untested is current drive. **No longer gates the board** — the
   jumper-selectable 75/50 Ω input termination covers either outcome (§6).
9. **AC volts between grounds** — CM4↔Extron chassis is the one still unmeasured.
   F9T↔CM4 read <10 mV and Teensy↔CM4 0.6 mV on 2026-09-09 (§3). Take the readings
   with the 10 MHz coax disconnected as well as connected; the difference is the
   actual size of the earth-path effect. Laptop on battery or unplugged — a
   mains-connected laptop adds its own earth path through USB. Belongs here rather
   than at the bench: the earth topology that matters is the rack's.
10. **Antenna:** confirm where the coax shield is bonded; if roof-mounted, a
    gas-discharge arrestor at the entry point. A GNSS DC block / galvanic isolator
    removes a whole class of ground problem, provided it passes the active antenna's
    bias. Also settles whether the antenna run — and therefore the stored CFG-TP5
    delay — changes at the move (§6b).

### Deferred — waiting on hardware that isn't here

11. **CLK4 amplitude / CM4 XIN receiver** (§8). Needs a second identical CM4 module
    to experiment on, and the real output is a proper circuit at the CM4 end rather
    than a resolved probe reading. Not chased on the running rig. Feeds the §6 scope
    question.
12. **The squarer/divider board itself.** Blocked only on the §6 scope question
    (enclosure consolidation, feed point, 54 MHz drive), not on any measurement.

### Connector construction note

At 10 MHz the wavelength in coax is ~20 m, so a few centimetres of unmatched junction
is electrically invisible and home-made parts are not a compromise. A small axial
metal-film resistor carries a few nH of parasitic inductance — j0.3 Ω against 75 Ω at
10 MHz. An 0805 soldered straight across a BNC is better still.

What matters is the **value, not the RF construction**: measure the resistor on the
DMM and use the measured figure in the arithmetic. 75.0 Ω exists in E96, but a
measured 74.3 Ω used knowingly beats a nominal 75 Ω used blindly.

Build the terminators as **male plugs** rather than feedthrough barrels where there is
a choice — a plug goes on the free leg of the tee, which is where it is wanted, and
doubles as a load elsewhere. Make the 50 Ω one at the same time for the
bench-instrument leg. Keep the leads short and the resistor body inside the shell.

Still worth buying eventually, but no longer blocking anything: a proper 75 Ω
feedthrough (the SDS1104X-E is 1 MΩ fixed, no switchable 50 Ω) and an SMA attenuator
kit (1/2/3/6/10 dB) for general bench work.

---

## 8. Things known to be out of spec but working

Kept here so they aren't "fixed" without thought.

- **54 MHz into CM4 XIN at full 3.3 V CMOS swing** through 10 Ω. Crystal inputs
  generally want ~1 Vpp AC-coupled. Stable for years.

  Measured 2026-09-09: **3.4 Vpp** at CLK4 `[meas]` — full rail plus overshoot from
  an unterminated line. An earlier reading of 1.22 Vpp / 394 mV rms was taken at a
  different point or under different loading; it was initially explained as the
  scope's 100 MHz bandwidth rounding a 54 MHz square, but that cannot account for
  both figures on the same instrument, so that explanation is withdrawn. The
  difference is load, not filtering. Worth resolving which probe point gave which,
  since a genuinely loaded 1.22 Vpp would imply XIN presents a far lower impedance
  than a crystal input nominally does — likely the internal Pierce amplifier
  running. `[?]`

  **Deferred, deliberately.** Not being chased on this rig. Characterising XIN
  properly means loading and probing an input that the whole system depends on, and
  the useful output is a designed receiver circuit at the CM4 end rather than a
  settled scope reading. Both want a **second identical CM4 module** to experiment
  on. Until one turns up this stays as it is — it has worked for years — and the
  board is laid out to anticipate it (§6).

  For PPS timestamping accuracy this path is close to irrelevant anyway — a few ps
  of jitter on the CPU clock disappears under interrupt latency. It matters for boot
  stability and for system clock frequency accuracy at long tau, not for edge
  capture.
- ~~**AR-40A into the Extron at 2.75 Vpp** vs 1.5 Vpp max.~~ **Resolved 2026-09-07**
  by the 7.01 dB pad; the DA now sees 1.245 Vpp. Kept as history — the years spent
  overdriven are the reason item 8 in §7 is still worth measuring.
- **Zero-centred sine into Si5351 CLKIN — CONFIRMED BY MEASUREMENT 2026-09-09.**
  `[meas]` Fed 2.49 Vpp unterminated from a DA output, CLKIN measures **2.1 Vpp /
  776 mV rms, visibly asymmetric**. That is the predicted clamping: the positive peak
  sits near +1.25 V unclamped while the negative is held around −0.85 V by the input
  protection diode plus source impedance, summing to 2.1 Vpp instead of 2.49. Crest
  factor 2.705 vs 2.828 for a clean sine — one half compressed.

  So the protection diode conducts every cycle and is acting as the level shifter,
  against a −0.5 V absolute maximum. Functionally it has worked for years, and the
  Si5351's own PLL contributes far more jitter than this does. Still worth fixing on
  the new board for two reasons: sustained current through a protection diode is not
  what it is for, and the threshold crossing is set by a diode's forward
  characteristic, which drifts with temperature rather than sitting at a fixed bias.
  This is the measured justification for the §6 input stage.
